A||||19||Jimmy||What brand of sway bar should I get? & where?||||1||
Z||000000||Jimmy||12-07-2000||07:03 PM||||Hey fellow Fiero Forum Members!  <BR>I need to let my brother know where to buy a rear sway bar for my 87 GT Fiero.  I have the stock front sway bar on the front(have not upgraded).  I haven't really looked around and need to know soon where he should order one from for Christmas.  Any help would be appreciated.  TIA<P>Jim <BR>87 GT T-TOP V8<BR>87 GT||||reg||1
Z||000001||Oreif||12-07-2000||07:15 PM||||Addco 7/8" at JCWhitney and other places. ($119.00 I think)<BR>KONI 1" at Fiero Store.<BR>(I bought the KONI 1" I think it's $139.00 with poly bushings and swaybar links)<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 12-07-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Jimmy||12-07-2000||07:19 PM||||Does the swaybar at JCWhitney come with end links? Poly?<BR>Would the 1" from the fiero store be too big?<BR>Sorry about all the questions, I'm searching the Forum for old posts right now.  My bro kinda just told me he'd like to order it by the weekend.  Thanks again<P>Jim||||reg||1
Z||000003||Ed||12-07-2000||07:25 PM||||I have an '87 GT.  Rear sway bar from Whitney.  Works fine.<P>They had the poly sets when I got mine.  Get poly for the front too, and upgrade.  Order poly separately, not included with the bar.    ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Oreif||12-08-2000||03:06 AM||||7/8" is great on a stock suspension. If you lower and stiffen the 1" would be better.<BR>Price wise they are close to the same. (Fiero Store KONI's come with all the bushings, Addco doesn't)||||reg||1
Z||000005||msweldon||12-08-2000||10:22 AM||||I could be wrong, but I have the Koni suspension setup from IRM which I believe is the same one from the fierostore.  The shocks are Koni's, however the springs are Suspension Tech's and the bar's are addco or some other brand. I believe.  I didn't think koni made bars..<P>I could be wrong....<P>marc||||reg||1
Z||000006||Oreif||12-08-2000||11:37 AM||||The sway bars (front and rear) that I purchased from the Fiero Store are printed on the one side as "KONI" and what looks like a date code or P/N. The Addco rear sway has definate angles that clear the exhaust on the right side the KONI has a more rounded bend.<P><BR>WOW, I just Emailed KONI about their sway bars, They don't make them. They buy them from Suspension Technics and stamp them when they sell the complete car kits. So my sway bars are made by them. According to KONI they use their (ST's) springs and sways with KONI shocks. <P>Ya learn something new every day!<P><p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 12-08-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||PlasticWrapd||12-09-2000||02:04 PM||||Many of you will argue with me about this, but...<P>I wouldn't recommend getting a large sway bar for the rear of the Fiero if you're an average driver.  The large bar will completely change the handling characteristics of the car, especially in wet weather.  The car will be much less "forgiving" in emergency situations.  Sure, the car will handle like it's on a set of rails, but will give no warning before the rear wheels lose traction and allow the car to go into a spin.<P>If you want to improve the handling while maintaining a decent margin of safety, install a front sway bar on the rear.  It will reduce the understeer and make the car handle better without making the car dangerous to drive. <P>Those of you with large rear bars will probably thumb your nose at me for this post.  You have to realize that you are accustomed to the handling of you car and probably know it's limitations.  The average driver would not and would probably get into trouble if he or she is not careful.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Oreif||12-09-2000||11:54 PM||||I have added a larger rear sway bar to my car and in the rain or slick surfaces I have had no problems. Once you add the rear sway bar, the tires on the car remain in contact with the road better on both sides of the car. Using a good set of performance/all-weather tires makes the difference. <BR>I have driven my car with and without the rear sway in the rain within 2 weeks of each ride and I prefer the drivability with the rear sway bar. ||||reg||1
Z||000009||webbee||12-10-2000||01:15 AM||||I have to vote with OREIF on this one. Sway bars are one of the best mods you can make to the Fiero or any other car.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Jimmy||12-10-2000||02:07 AM||||I've heard form racers that a bigger bar on back than front can be VERY bad.  I've heard the front should always be slightly larger.  Anyone else with any thoughts on this?<P>Jim <BR>87 GT T-TOP V8<BR>87 GT    ||||reg||1
Z||000011||GarethWright||12-10-2000||02:08 AM||||my '87 GT came with some sort of sway bar on the front and back.  I did feel the rear end start to go once while driving on the turnpike in Really heavy rain (like Really low visibility Hard Rain) but I let off the gas and all went back into line. No more problems after that.  As Plasticwrapd said, I don't know what the car feels like without the  bars on it, and I'm used to them by now, so I cant really compare to stock, but driving in good weather or after rain (slick streets), I have  more traction and control than in my previous cars though those did not include a Fiero.||||reg||1
Z||000012||PlasticWrapd||12-10-2000||02:33 AM||||Yes, the rear sway bar "plants" the tires and yes, they really grip.  The handling is awesome, I must agree.  BUT (you knew that "but" was coming, didn't you?), when the tires are "planted" it takes much more force to cause them to lose traction.  So when you finally exceed the amount of lateral force that the tires can absorb they lose traction and skid.  Since this force is now, lets say, twice the normal amount of force than without the bar, you are now sliding around at twice the speed.  The real problem is there is no warning when the tires are about to lose traction.  They just break loose and you slide.  Now, without the bar, the body rolls and places more force on the outside tire.  The inside tire loses traction first and you "feel" it before the other tire loses traction.  This gives you plenty of time to correct and keep from totally losing control.  See what I mean?  What I am trying to say is a little body roll and understeer is better for the average driver.  They will have more time to react in an imminent skid situation.  As for those who have driven with the larger bar for some time, you probably are quite aware of the car's limitations and know when to back off.||||reg||1
Z||000013||Oreif||12-10-2000||10:29 AM||||Plasticwrapd, I see what you are saying.<BR> Just to clearify, (like Jimmy stated) If you are talking about putting a larger sway bar in back that is physically larger than the front, it will mess up handling. In both wet or dry (it will actually cause a worse understeer condition). <P>If you are just talking the increased lateral push, I increased my front sway bar from 1" to a 1 1/4" and I put a 1" in the back. I have also added poly bushings, lowering springs, and KYB shocks/struts. This has forced me to go from a 60 series tire down to a 50 series performance tire. Even on dry pavment the suspension forced the higher side walls to roll slightly, Hence I needed a lower stiffer sidewall to decrease tire roll due to a higher lateral push. If an average driver who doesn't normally push his vehicle keeps original tires on and isn't ready for it than yes it could be a <B> potential </B> problem.<P>Whenever I do a mod to the suspension, tires or brakes I usually find an empty parking lot and check it out. You don't need to drive 70mph to check out handling of a car.<BR>You can also do this in the rain too. The other thing about the "parking lot check" is you will also be able to better judge the vehicles limits or at least evaluate the mod to see if you like it. <P>Maybe we need to start a thread of brake/suspension mods and how they affect the car in "real world" daily driving or adverse weather. That way those who are just beginning to get into the more "aggresive" driving can evaluate wether they want to do a particular mod or not. (larger brakes can cause the front brakes to lock up sooner than expected in rain/snow)<P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg">    <P>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 12-10-2000).]<p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 12-10-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000014||Greg Piet||12-10-2000||02:03 PM||||My rear sway bar isnt as large as my front sway bar, but I must concur with plastic wrapd...   In wet weather (which amounts to wet patches of road in las vegas from peoples lawn care)my back end is much more twitchy with a rear swaybar...  <P>Plasticwrapd...   that term twice the normal force screwed me up...   It took me a minute to determine which "normal force" you were referring to...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000015||PlasticWrapd||12-11-2000||02:05 AM||||Sorry...   Hmmm... perhaps bad sentance (sentence?) structure.  This is by no means a "real world" situation, just an imaginary condition to get the point across...<P>With a given set of tires, lets say it takes 130 lbs of lateral force to "break 'em loose" and cause a skid.  Now you add the bar to "plant" the tires and it now takes 260 lbs of lateral force to cause a skid. <P>This is what I meant by "normal" force (130) and twice the force (260).<BR>Sorry for the confusion.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000016||PlasticWrapd||12-11-2000||02:28 AM||||Understeer vs. Oversteer...<P>Actually, the Fiero lacks the rear bar to induce understeer (the rear of the car being a bit lazy in relation to the front).  This is so "Joe Average Driver" doesn't go into a spin every time he turns a corner above 20 mph.  With no bar, the tire on the inside loses traction and the rear of the car tends to "walk" sideways in the opposite direction of the turn and the front "plows" into the turn because of the rear end action.<P>Now add the bar.  The tires stay more firmly planted and there is less lean.  The rear end tracks more accurately and turns tighter with the front.  The car has less body roll and the outer front tire is no longer carrying 90% of the weight of the car and is less likely to "plow" into the turn.  The rear tracks more accurately and the car is now "oversteering" in relation to having no bar.<P>I think that the larger bar in front theory works with front engine cars.  The larger bar is needed to compensate for the engine's weight being right over the front tires.<P>I have a rear bar (a front bar on the rear) and I have experienced conditions where the front tires lose traction while the rear are firmly planted.  Try doing a quick 90 degree turn at about 30 mph.  The front tires turn, but the car keeps going straight!!!!  Kinda scary if you ask me!  too much oversteer???||||reg||1
Z||000017||fiero56||12-11-2000||04:30 PM||||So why did '88 GT and Formula come factory with rear sway bar?||||reg||1
Z||000018||Zorba||12-12-2000||12:24 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by fiero56:<BR><B>So why did '88 GT and Formula come factory with rear sway bar?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>They finally got a clue at GM and budgets.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000019||lowCG||12-12-2000||07:19 PM||||I've tried pretty hard to keep the back of my car compliant to keep the snap-oversteer-thing from ruining my day on some wet/icy road,what I wound up with was a 3/4" rear bar,mounted with heim ends,and the front bar hung on poly inner bushings.The springs are right around 305# in front,and 330# in back.Now it'll oversteer only when provoked,and is nuetral the rest of the time.||||reg||1
